PR #689  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: ACLIC sourcecfg child_index corrected from 1 to 0: software following the old spec would program an invalid child domain index, causing delegated interrupts to be silently lost
  [RISK] include/linux/irqchip/riscv-aplic.h:20-24  (header definition)
      APLIC_SOURCECFG_CHILDIDX_MASK (0x3ff) is defined but referenced nowhere else in the codebase — no read-modify-write path, no validation, no helper that applies this mask. This means every code path that writes sourcecfg destroys the child_index field silently. The infrastructure exists on paper but the semantic gap is total: the kernel has no mechanism to preserve, validate, or route based on the child-domain index. Any delegation configuration in hardware is invisible to and unprotected by software.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_aplic.c:67-70  aplic_write_sourcecfg
      When the guest OS writes sourcecfg with D=1 (delegation requested), the KVM emulation unconditionally sets val=0, which forces D=0, child_index=0, and SM=INACTIVE. The interrupt source becomes inactive in the root domain and delegation is silently cancelled. When D=0, val is masked to SM_MASK only, also stripping child_index. In both paths, the child_index field is destroyed — the KVM APLIC has no code path that preserves or uses child_index for interrupt routing. This matches the specification's vulnerability pattern: any attempt to configure interrupt delegation results in the interrupt being silently lost rather than forwarded to the child domain.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-aplic-main.c:228-232  aplic_irq_set_type
      Constructs a val containing only SM bits (0-7) from the switch statement and writes it directly to the hardware sourcecfg register via writel() without a prior readl(). Any pre-existing D bit or CHILDIDX configuration written by firmware is silently overwritten with zeroes. The in-memory struct is also updated with the SM-only value, making the loss permanent across save/restore cycles. Currently safe only under the implicit (unenforced) invariant that firmware never pre-configures delegation in sourcecfg registers before the kernel boots.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-aplic-main.c:296-297  aplic_init_hw_irqs
      Boot-time init writes 0 to every sourcecfg register (i=1 to nr_irqs). If firmware pre-configured delegation (D=1, child_index set) in any sourcecfg register before the kernel boots, this loop unconditionally wipes those settings — creating a boot-time window where interrupt delegation is destroyed. Safe only if the firmware guarantee 'no delegation is configured at boot' holds, which is not enforced or documented in code.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_aplic.c:213-215  aplic_read_input
      When sourcecfg has the D (delegate) bit set, the function jumps to 'skip' and returns false unconditionally — the interrupt is treated as having no pending input regardless of actual hardware state. Combined with aplic_write_sourcecfg forcing child_index=0 when D=1, this means any delegated interrupt source is permanently invisible to the KVM APLIC. The specification's vulnerability pattern is directly realized: delegated interrupts cannot be observed or delivered.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_aplic.c:290-293  kvm_riscv_aia_aplic_inject
      This is the critical interrupt delivery path. When sourcecfg D bit is set, the function jumps to skip_unlock, bypassing all injection logic: SM-based level/edge handling, pending-bit set, and MSI delivery to the target hart are all skipped. A delegated interrupt (D=1, child_index set) is silently dropped — it is never forwarded to the child domain, never delivered as an MSI, and never sets any pending bit. This directly matches the specification's vulnerability pattern for ACLIC interrupt delivery: 'the delegated interrupt is routed to a non-existent domain index, causing it to be silently dropped rather than delivered to the intended supervisor-level handler.' The KVM lacks any child-domain emulation entirely, making this a structural gap rather than a simple off-by-one.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_aplic.c:465-468  aplic_mmio_write_offset
      The MMIO offset computation correctly maps to 1-based IRQ numbering: i = ((off - APLIC_SOURCECFG_BASE) >> 2) + 1. The range check (off < APLIC_SOURCECFG_BASE + (aplic->nr_irqs - 1) * 4) bounds IRQ indices to [1, nr_irqs-1]. The arithmetic itself is correct. However, this is the guest-visible entry point that dispatches to the buggy aplic_write_sourcecfg, so while the MMIO decode is compliant, it faithfully exposes the child_index-destroying behavior to the guest.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-aplic-main.c:66-83  aplic_save_states
      Suspend save path reads target (line 77) and interrupt-enable bits (line 82) from hardware into the in-memory struct, but never reads sourcecfg from hardware — there is no readl(regs + APLIC_SOURCECFG_BASE + i*4) call. If firmware wrote delegation state (D bit, child_index) to hardware sourcecfg registers, that state is not captured in the software snapshot and will be lost across a suspend/resume cycle. This is an asymmetric save: target and IE are preserved, but sourcecfg delegation state is silently dropped.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-aplic-main.c:45-48  aplic_restore_states
      Resume restore path writes saved sourcecfg from the in-memory struct back to hardware (line 47). Since the in-memory sourcecfg is populated only by irq_set_type (SM bits only, CHILDIDX=0, D=0) and never refreshed from hardware during apply_save_states, this restore perpetuates the zeroed child_index across suspend/resume cycles even if hardware had valid delegation state before suspend. The comment correctly notes that sourcecfg must be restored before target (per AIA spec), but the restoration data itself is incomplete.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-aplic-main.h:26-27  (struct definition)
      The aplic_src_ctrl struct stores sourcecfg as a plain u32 with no distinction between SM, D, and CHILDIDX sub-fields. This struct is the sole backing store for save/restore and is populated exclusively by irq_set_type (SM-only writes) and initialized to zero by devm_kzalloc. There is no code path that reads sourcecfg from hardware into this struct field. The struct design implicitly assumes sourcecfg only contains SM bits, but the AIA spec defines sourcecfg as a composite register with D and CHILDIDX fields that must be preserved for correct child-domain delegation.

