PR #689  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: ACLIC sourcecfg child_index corrected from 1 to 0: software following the old spec would program an invalid child domain index, causing delegated interrupts to be silently lost
  [BUG] lib/utils/fdt/fdt_helper.c:758-758  fdt_parse_aplic_node
      ROOT CAUSE: deleg->child_index is assigned the zero-based array index j from iterating over the DT 'riscv,children' phandle list. The AIA DT binding says riscv,children order equals hardware child-domain-index order, so on a correct DT this is fine. However, in practice on real hardware, the S-mode child APLIC phandle may appear at array position 1 while the hardware only has a single child domain (index 0). The code performs NO validation that the resulting child_index corresponds to a valid hardware child domain. An incorrect child_index causes the interrupt to be routed to a non-existent domain entry, silently dropping interrupts (DoS).
  [BUG] lib/utils/irqchip/aplic.c:162-165  aplic_init
      BUG MANIFESTATION: Writes (APLIC_SOURCECFG_D | deleg->child_index) directly to the sourcecfg CSR for each delegated IRQ. The child_index value in bits 9:0 (CHILDIDX) originates from the DT array index at fdt_helper.c:758 and may be semantically wrong (e.g., 1 instead of 0). The only guard is 'if (deleg->child_index > APLIC_SOURCECFG_CHILDIDX_MASK) continue' at line 155, which checks the bit-width bound (<=0x3ff) but does NOT check whether the value references a valid hardware child domain. A wrong value causes the interrupt controller to route to a non-existent domain.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-fast-interrupt/pr-689/

  [BUG] lib/utils/irqchip/aplic.c:194-199  aplic_reinit_all
