PR #689  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: ACLIC sourcecfg child_index corrected from 1 to 0: software following the old spec would program an invalid child domain index, causing delegated interrupts to be silently lost
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:119-136  aplic_handle_sourcecfg
      The MMIO write handler stores the guest-written value verbatim into irq->sourcecfg with zero field extraction or validation. When the guest sets SOURCECFG_D (bit 10, delegation enable) and writes a non-zero child_index (bits 0-9), the value is accepted without any check that child_index==0 (the only valid child domain). This is the sole entry point for guest-written sourcecfg values — any PR fixing child-index semantics must add validation here to reject or sanitize child_index != 0 when D=1.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:482-548  aplic_inject_irq
      When irq->sourcecfg has SOURCECFG_D set (delegation enabled), the function silently returns 0 at line 504 without: (a) reading the CHILD_INDEX field from sourcecfg, (b) asserting the pending bit, (c) forwarding the IRQ to the child domain, or (d) signalling any error. Delegated interrupts are unconditionally dropped. This is the downstream consequence of the missing child_index logic — even if child_index were corrected to 0, the interrupt would still be silently lost because no forwarding path exists. A fix must add child_index-based forwarding logic here, routing the interrupt to the supervisor-level child domain when D=1 and child_index=0.
  [OK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:59-74  (global macro definitions)
      The CSR field-layout macros are correctly defined per the RISC-V APLIC specification: SOURCECFG_D (bit 10) for delegation enable, SOURCECFG_CHILD_INDEX_S=0 and SOURCECFG_CHILD_INDEX_M=0x3ff for the child-domain index in bits 0-9 when D=1. However, no code in the file ever reads, validates, or uses SOURCECFG_CHILD_INDEX_M — the macros exist but are dead code. This confirms the semantic gap: the fields are defined but never consumed, so the child_index value is never extracted from sourcecfg at any point in the IRQ injection or delivery path.
  [OK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:275-279  aplic_mmio_access
      The dispatch-path index arithmetic i = ((reg - APLIC_SOURCECFG(1)) >> 2) + 1 correctly computes the 1-based interrupt source index from the register offset using the standard APLIC numbering convention (index 1 = first wired interrupt). This 1-based interrupt-source numbering is unrelated to the child domain index (which is a field within the sourcecfg CSR). The arithmetic is correct and does not need adjustment for a child_index fix.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_aplic.c:55-58  (global macro definitions)
      The DOMAINCFG macros define DOMAINCFG_DM (Direct Mode, bit 2) and DOMAINCFG_IE (Interrupt Enable, bit 8). The vmm code checks DOMAINCFG_IE at line 496 in aplic_inject_irq but never checks DOMAINCFG_DM when deciding delegation routing behavior. Per the APLIC spec, when DM=1 (direct mode), sourcecfg D=1 (delegation) is architecturally invalid — delegation to child domains is only meaningful in hierarchical (non-direct) mode. The code implicitly assumes the domain is never in direct mode when delegation is used, but this invariant is not enforced: the guest could write DM=1 via the domaincfg MMIO path while also setting D=1 in sourcecfg. In a complete fix for delegated interrupt routing, DOMAINCFG_DM should be checked alongside SOURCECFG_D to reject or handle the D=1 + DM=1 conflict.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:83-85  (global macro definitions)
      The host-side APLIC driver defines APLIC_SRC_CFG_DLGT (bit 10, delegation) — matching the vmm's SOURCECFG_D — and APLIC_SRC_CFG_SM_MASK covering bits 0-2. This SM_MASK overlaps the same bits that encode CHILD_INDEX in delegation mode, but this is not a bug: the host kernel never uses delegation mode (all sources are configured DETACHED at line 369-370), so the bit overlap is harmless. The host operates exclusively in direct mode.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/aplic.c:355-370  aplic_setup_direct_mode
      The host kernel APLIC driver configures exclusively direct mode (APLIC_MODE_DIRECT at line 356) and sets all interrupt sources to DETACHED (line 369-370). APLIC_SRC_CFG_DLGT is never set. This confirms the FreeBSD host kernel has no delegation/child-domain support, consistent with the absence of Smclic/ACLIC functionality. No bug here — it provides architectural context confirming that delegation is purely a vmm-level concern.


